   },
   "parent": "client-created-root",
   "children": [
    "0e05b5a5-b57e-4ddb-8d36-257eb4a32cec"
   ]
  },
  "0e05b5a5-b57e-4ddb-8d36-257eb4a32cec": {
   "id": "0e05b5a5-b57e-4ddb-8d36-257eb4a32cec",
   "message": {
    "id": "0e05b5a5-b57e-4ddb-8d36-257eb4a32cec",
    "author": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "name": null,
     "metadata": {}
    },
    "create_time": 1761615667.456,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "Turn the 20-point row into explicit levels so you're not re-deciding every paper:\n\n| Points | What it looks like |\n|---|---|\n| 18–20 | Names a framework **and** uses its concepts to reach a conclusion about this case (e.g., runs the utilitarian calculation across the stakeholders they identified; or tests the action against Kant's universalizability). Acknowledges a limitation of the framework. |\n| 14–17 | Applies the framework to the case, but unevenly: some steps asserted rather than reasoned, or ignores one major stakeholder. |\n| 10–13 | Names and defines the framework correctly; application is a sentence or two of \"this is unethical under deontology\" with little reasoning. |\n| 5–9 | Names a framework but misdefines it, or the \"application\" is unrelated to the framework's actual logic. |\n| 0–4 | No framework, or only a dictionary-style definition. |\n\nThe line between 10–13 and 14–17 is the one you'll hit most: *does the conclusion depend on the framework's reasoning, or would they have said the same thing without it?*\n\nWrite 2–3 anchor papers (one per band) and grade against them. That keeps the 40th paper consistent with the 4th."
     ]
    },
